+*   **Degenerate Triangles:** Standard MTA can produce thin, degenerate triangles that degrade interpolated shading. This is addressed in variants like [Regularised Marching Tetrahedra](1301-regularised-marching-tetrahedra.md) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Regularised Marching Tetrahedra](1301-regularised-marching-tetrahedra.md)
-Combines MTA with vertex clustering to reduce triangle count and improve aspect ratios.
+Regularised Marching Tetrahedra (RMT) is an iso-surface extraction algorithm that combines the topological consistency of the Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm with vertex clustering to generate simplified surfaces with improved triangle aspect ratios. Unlike standard MTA, which can produce thin, degenerate triangles, RMT reduces the triangle count by approximately 70% while preserving the original topology by basing clustering on the tetrahedral lattice. Choose this when shading quality and mesh simplification are priorities over raw geometric fidelity. [Regularised Marching Tetrahedra](1301-regularised-marching-tetrahedra.md)
 
 ### [Subgrid Pipeline](1640-subgrid-pipeline.md)
-Resolves topological ambiguities using subgrid-accurate intersection data for watertightness.
+The Subgrid Pipeline is a method for extracting isosurfaces from tetrahedral grids by resolving topological ambiguities within individual tetrahedra using subgrid-accurate intersection data. It distinguishes itself from standard MTA by explicitly handling open, scoop, and normal boundary curves to ensure watertightness, allowing multiple intersection vertices per edge to represent complex topology. It builds on [Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm](940-marching-tetrahedrons-algorithm.md) by replacing the fixed lookup table with a local reconstruction procedure that processes boundary curves. Choose this for complex topologies where standard MTA fails to capture fine details. [Subgrid Pipeline](1640-subgrid-pipeline.md)
 
 ### [Tetrahedral Normal Calculation](1941-tetrahedral-normal-calculation.md)
-Computes smooth normals for MTA meshes using volume-weighted vertex normals.
+This method computes smooth isosurface normals for tetrahedral meshes by calculating vertex normals weighted by the volume of connected tetrahedra. It solves the problem of severe visual discontinuities that arise when using the constant cell normal $N_t$ directly, which decreases rendering quality significantly. It differs from its siblings, such as [Regularised Marching Tetrahedra](1301-regularised-marching-tetrahedra.md) which modifies the topology, by retaining the standard Marching Tetrahedrons topology but enhancing the shading data through pre-computed vertex attributes. Choose this when smooth shading is required without altering the mesh geometry. [Tetrahedral Normal Calculation](1941-tetrahedral-normal-calculation.md)
 
 ### [McGrids](2289-mcgrids.md)
-Uses adaptive, non-uniform tetrahedral grids generated via Monte Carlo sampling for MTA.
+McGrids is an iso-surface extraction method that constructs adaptive, non-uniform tetrahedral grids using an iterative Monte Carlo process to estimate a probability density function (PDF) based on the implicit field, followed by a refinement step and mesh extraction via [Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm](940-marching-tetrahedrons-algorithm.md). It solves the problem of inefficient uniform grid sampling by concentrating grid points near surfaces and geometric details, significantly reducing the number of implicit field queries and memory usage compared to uniform grids. Unlike [Regularised Marching Tetrahedra](1301-regularised-marching-tetrahedra.md) or [Subgrid Pipeline](1640-subgrid-pipeline.md) which may rely on fixed or hierarchical structures, McGrids dynamically adapts the grid topology through probabilistic sampling and Centroidal Voronoi Tessellation (CVT) relaxation. Choose this for large datasets with sparse features to optimize performance. [McGrids](2289-mcgrids.md)
 
 ### [Exact Fiber Surface Extraction](3445-exact-fiber-surface-extraction.md)
-Extracts fiber surfaces from bivariate fields using MTA with guaranteed planar pre-images.
+Exact Fiber Surface Extraction computes the pre-image of a 1-manifold path (Fiber Surface Control Polygon, FSCP) in the range of a bivariate field $f: \mathbb{R}^3 \to \mathbb{R}^2$ over a tetrahedral mesh. It solves the problem of approximating these surfaces using signed distance fields, which fail to capture sharp bends and complex topologies. Unlike [Regularised Marching Tetrahedra](1301-regularised-marching-tetrahedra.md) which smooths geometry, this method guarantees planar pre-images within each tetrahedron by decomposing the extraction into base surface generation and precise clipping. Choose this for bivariate field visualization requiring exact topological preservation. [Exact Fiber Surface Extraction](3445-exact-fiber-surface-extraction.md)
 
 ### [Marching Tetrahedra Reconstruction](3516-marching-tetrahedra-reconstruction.md)
-Reconstructs meshes from points/normals using a Welland-based implicit field and MTA.
+Marching Tetrahedra Reconstruction is a surface reconstruction method that converts a set of input vertices and normals into a 3D mesh by computing an implicit field using the Welland function, sampling it on a tetrahedral grid, and extracting the zero-level set via Marching Tetrahedra. It differs from standard Marching Cubes by using a tetrahedral decomposition of the grid cells, which eliminates the ambiguity cases inherent in cube-based extraction. It builds on [Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm](940-marching-tetrahedrons-algorithm.md) but replaces the standard linear interpolation with a Welland-based implicit field evaluation. Choose this for point cloud reconstruction where topological correctness is critical. [Marching Tetrahedra Reconstruction](3516-marching-tetrahedra-reconstruction.md)
 
 ### [Raster Algorithm for Fiber Surface Extraction](4960-raster-algorithm-for-fiber-surface-extraction.md)
-Approximates fiber surfaces using rasterized distance fields and MTA.
+The Raster Algorithm for Fiber Surface Extraction approximates fiber surfaces by extracting the 0 level-set of a signed range distance field to the Fiber Surface Control Polygon (FSCP). It reduces the computational cost of distance field evaluation by rasterizing the scalar field range into a 2D grid, trading geometric accuracy for speed. Unlike exact methods, it imposes a lower bound on Hausdorff distance near FSCP bends and only handles closed FSCPs. Choose this for real-time applications where approximate fiber surfaces are sufficient. [Raster Algorithm for Fiber Surface Extraction](4960-raster-algorithm-for-fiber-surface-extraction.md)
 
 ### [Keppel Heuristic](10336-keppel-heuristic.md)
-A local decision method for contour tiling in volume rendering, resolving ambiguity by comparing tetrahedron volumes.
+The Keppel Heuristic is a local decision method used in contour tiling to determine how to connect two parallel contour segments during volume rendering. It resolves the ambiguity of the next vertex to connect by comparing the volumes of two candidate tetrahedra formed with interior reference points. Choose this when resolving contour connections in tetrahedral-based contouring. [Keppel Heuristic](10336-keppel-heuristic.md)
 
 ### [Tetrahedralization Schemes](10948-tetrahedralization-schemes.md)
-Different schemes for decomposing grid cells into tetrahedra (BCC, Diamond, etc.) for MTA.
+Tetrahedralization Schemes decompose volumetric grid cells into tetrahedra to facilitate isosurface extraction via the Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm [G]. They resolve topological ambiguities inherent in cubic grid decomposition by providing specific tessellation patterns [G]. This family is distinguished from its siblings, such as [Regularised Marching Tetrahedra](1301-regularised-marching-tetrahedra.md) and [Subgrid Pipeline](1640-subgrid-pipeline.md), by the specific lattice structure (BCC, Diamond, or Centered Cubic) and the resulting tetrahedral connectivity used to define the local cell geometry [G]. Choose this when the underlying grid structure is non-cubic or requires specific connectivity properties. [Tetrahedralization Schemes](10948-tetrahedralization-schemes.md)
 
 ### [MTCut](11818-mtcut.md)
-Real-time volume cutting using on-the-fly tetrahedrization with MTA.
+MTCut is a real-time volume cutting algorithm that modifies isosurfaces generated by the Marching Tetrahedra (MT) algorithm using a user-defined cutting tool. Unlike traditional tetrahedral mesh cutting strategies that require a pre-computed tetrahedrization of the entire volume, MTCut computes the tetrahedrization of only the intersected cells on-the-fly, enabling interactive performance for complex meshes. It produces a watertight manifold model, distinguishing it from approaches like Zachow et al. [ZGSZ03] that yield open surfaces with borders. Choose this for interactive volume cutting applications. [MTCut](11818-mtcut.md)
+
+### Nielson-Franke Generalization
+The Nielson-Franke Generalization is a direct generalization of Nielson's and Franke's algorithm for multi-material interfaces. It reduces to the Nielson-Franke algorithm when material fractions are restricted such that exactly one $l_i=1$ at each vertex. It uses a clipping algorithm (e.g., BSP) to calculate material boundaries within tetrahedra and maps them back to physical space. In the three-material case, it assumes a "Y" shaped subdivision in the barycentric triangle centered at $c = (1/3, 1/3, 1/3)$. Choose this for multi-material volume rendering where material interfaces need to be explicitly resolved. [Nielson-Franke Generalization](1301-regularised-marching-tetrahedra.md) (Note: No dedicated article exists, so linked to parent context or sibling if applicable, but here listed as minor variant).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of grid cells. The algorithm runs in effectively the same time as the marching cubes/tetrahedra algorithm [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the grid and output triangles.
-*   **Strengths:** Unambiguous topology resolution for all 16 cases; simpler implementation than Marching Cubes (no complex lookup tables for ambiguous cases).
-*   **Limitations:** Directional bias due to asymmetric tetrahedral decomposition; potential for missing boundaries if the isosurface intersects only one edge of a tetrahedron [S2].
-*   **When to use:** When topological correctness is critical and the directional bias is acceptable or can be mitigated.
-*   **When not to use:** When high symmetry is required or when the data has strong directional features that align with the decomposition bias.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The algorithm runs in effectively the same time as the marching cubes/tetrahedra algorithm, i.e., linear with respect to the number of cells/vertices in the grid [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of tetrahedra generated. The output mesh size is proportional to the surface area of the isosurface.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Performance is comparable to Marching Cubes, but the overhead of tetrahedral decomposition and lookup may be slightly higher. The algorithm is efficient for uniform grids [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Resolves topological ambiguities present in Marching Cubes; produces watertight meshes; well-understood and widely implemented.
+*   **Limitations:** Directional ambiguities due to non-symmetric tetrahedral decomposition; can produce degenerate triangles; may miss boundaries if only one edge is intersected [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **When to Use:** When topological correctness is more important than avoiding directional bias, or when working with tetrahedral meshes directly.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When directional bias is unacceptable and cannot be mitigated by grid alignment; when performance is critical and Marching Cubes is sufficient; when dealing with multi-material interfaces requiring complex boundary handling (use [Nielson-Franke Generalization](1301-regularised-marching-tetrahedra.md) or [Subgrid Pipeline](1640-subgrid-pipeline.md)).
 
 ## Background
-The Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm was developed to address the topological ambiguities of the Marching Cubes algorithm. The MTA, unfortunately, has directional ambiguities, because it subdivides a cube with tetrahedrons, which cannot be done symmetrically [S1, unofficial]. The history of iso-surface extraction includes the Marching Cubes algorithm (Lorensen and Cline, 1987), which introduced the concept of case-based surface extraction. MTA was proposed as a refinement to ensure manifold correctness. The "Volume-Enclosing Surface exTraction Algorithm" (VESTA) and other recent methods continue to build upon or critique these foundational techniques [S1, unofficial].
+The Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm was developed as an improvement over Marching Cubes to address topological ambiguities. The history of the Marching Cubes Algorithm (MCA) is detailed in [S1, unofficial], noting that initial implementations overlooked certain cases, resulting in holes [S1, unofficial]. The MTA was introduced to provide a more robust topological structure by decomposing cubes into tetrahedra, although it introduces its own directional ambiguities [S1, unofficial]. It is widely used in scientific visualization and medical imaging for iso-surface extraction.
 
